@@ -37,7 +37,7 @@ A session claims a worktree when:
 
 The mod checks the working directory and the project root at each update and every 15 seconds while a row is shown. It checks the folders of the last 64 files the session wrote at each update, so a worktree made and written in the same turn is claimed once git lists it. Subagents' calls count, but a subagent's own working directory does not.
 
-Claims only grow: a worktree stays claimed for the rest of the session, even after the session moves away. A session that claims no worktree saves nothing. Each call to the session or the store gives up after 2 seconds; a call that gives up claims nothing that update. They survive `/clear`. The mod saves them in its store, keyed by the session id, so `--resume` brings them back. A saved entry not updated for 30 days is deleted. The mod looks for such entries at a session's first update that reads the session's own entry, and stops looking 2 seconds after it starts. So the first update waits at most about 6 seconds for the store: 2 for the session's own entry, 2 for the clean-up, 2 to save its claims. All three limits rely on the engine's timer; if the engine never fires it, a store call that never answers holds the update. The mod skips an entry it cannot read, or that reads as unset (no value). Sessions usually start at different entries, so one entry that never answers does not stop every session's clean-up.
+Claims only grow: a worktree stays claimed for the rest of the session, even after the session moves away. A session that claims no worktree saves nothing. Each call to the session or the store gives up after 2 seconds; a call that gives up claims nothing that update. They survive `/clear`. The mod saves them in its store, keyed by the session id, so `--resume` brings them back. A saved entry not updated for 30 days is deleted. The mod looks for such entries at a session's first update that reads the session's own entry, and stops looking 2 seconds after it starts. So the first update waits at most about 6 seconds for the store: 2 for the session's own entry, 2 for the clean-up, 2 to save its claims. Every 2-second limit relies on the engine's timer; if the engine never fires it, a session or store call that never answers holds the update. A session call that gives up counts as one that failed: the mod keeps the last working directory it knew, and draws nothing that update when it knows no working directory at all, or cannot learn when the session started or, with `context` on, how full the context is. So a session that never answers holds each Write, Edit, NotebookEdit or Bash call 2 seconds before it runs. One update asks the session at most eight things, so it waits at most 16 seconds for the session, on top of its waits for git, files and the store. The mod skips an entry it cannot read, or that reads as unset (no value). Sessions usually start at different entries, so one entry that never answers does not stop every session's clean-up.
 
 The *current* worktree is the one that holds the working directory, or else the one that holds the project root. Its rows come first. Rows from the other claimed worktrees start with `other tree: `.
 
